# cobalt-dingo environment configuration.
#
# Two distinct Fortnox connected apps are configured here, one per mode.
# FORTNOX_MODE selects which credential set + token file is used at runtime;
# Taskfile targets set it explicitly so a forgotten env var cannot route a
# command to the wrong Fortnox company.
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored.

# ── Fortnox mode ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Selected automatically by `task <target>` targets (mcp:sandbox and
# fortnox:check:sandbox set sandbox; mcp:production and
# fortnox:check:production set production). When running a binary directly
# with `go run`, set this manually:
#
#   sandbox        — write-capable; for development against the Fortnox sandbox
#   production     — the live Fortnox company. Read-only unless
#                    FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_ALLOW_WRITES=true, which nothing sets.
#                    The local gate refuses non-GET; note there is NO read-only
#                    OAuth scope to back it up (see the production section).

FORTNOX_MODE=

# ── Sandbox connected app ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Register a separate connected app in the Fortnox developer portal pointing
# at the sandbox. This app SHOULD have full scopes since seed/teardown writes.

FORTNOX_SANDBOX_CLIENT_ID=
FORTNOX_SANDBOX_CLIENT_SECRET=
FORTNOX_SANDBOX_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8080/callback
FORTNOX_SANDBOX_SCOPES="companyinformation bookkeeping customer invoice supplier supplierinvoice payment currency project costcenter assets settings"
FORTNOX_SANDBOX_INVOICE_INBOX=

# ── Production connected app ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Register a SECOND connected app pointing at your live Fortnox company.
#
# WARNING — there is no such thing as a read-only Fortnox scope. Fortnox's own
# developer documentation says so without qualification:
#
#   "All scopes gives both read and write access to an endpoint and it is not
#    possible to only have read access through the API."
#   -- https://www.fortnox.se/developer/guides-and-good-to-know/scopes
#      (retrieved 2026-09-08)
#
# Scopes are RESOURCE-scoped, not verb-scoped: granting `supplierinvoice`
# grants read AND write on supplier invoices. No portal setting, scope suffix
# or per-scope permission changes that.
#
# Full scope set, so you can pick the narrowest grant deliberately:
#   archive article assets bookkeeping connectfile costcenter currency customer
#   developerapi inbox invoice noxfinansinvoice offer order payment price print
#   profile project salary settings supplier supplierinvoice timereporting
#   warehouse warehousecustomdocument
#
# This file previously claimed the opposite ("Fortnox itself will refuse writes
# at the API gateway. Belt and braces."). That was wrong, and it mattered:
# ADR-0001 was written leaning on it. See ADR-0001's 2026-09-08 amendment.
#
# What that means in practice:
#   - In production the CLIENT-SIDE gate is the only thing preventing writes.
#     It is enforced in three places and pinned by tests
#     (TestProductionWiring_* in internal/adapter/fortnox).
#   - The only real lever Fortnox gives you is WHICH RESOURCES to grant. Grant
#     the narrowest set the read paths actually need, and accept that each one
#     carries write capability with it.
#   - Do NOT grant `payment`. Nothing in the read-only flows needs it, and it is
#     the highest-consequence resource in the list.
#
# Narrow set for the read-only live-data work (docs/live-financial-data.md):
#   companyinformation, bookkeeping, supplier, supplierinvoice, invoice, customer
# Add project / costcenter / assets only if you want those MCP tools to work.

FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_CLIENT_ID=
FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_CLIENT_SECRET=
FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8080/callback
FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_SCOPES="companyinformation bookkeeping supplier supplierinvoice invoice customer"
FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_INVOICE_INBOX=

# Writes against the live company are refused unless this is exactly "true".
# Leave it UNSET. Turning it on requires an ADR superseding ADR-0001, not a
# config change — see docs/adr/0001-read-only-live-financial-data.md.
# FORTNOX_PRODUCTION_ALLOW_WRITES=

# ── Application ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PORT=8080
DATABASE_URL=

# ── Authentication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The server REFUSES TO START unless it has an authenticator. There is no
# production config that turns this off: an unauthenticated server starts
# perfectly well and answers every request with a 200, so nothing downstream —
# not the liveness probe, not the ingress, not a human reading the pod status —
# reports the difference. Crashing is the only failure mode that gets noticed.
#
# Configure OIDC (all four), or opt out explicitly for local development.
# The opt-out applies ONLY when no issuer is configured. An issuer that IS set
# and fails to initialise is fatal regardless — that is the production case
# (co-boot race against Authentik) this guards. See cmd/server/wiring.go.

OIDC_ISSUER_URL=
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=
SESSION_SECRET=

# Development only. Serves every route publicly.
# COBALT_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED=true

# ── Debtor (PAIN.001 batch generator) ───────────────────────────────────────

COBALT_DEBTOR_NAME=
COBALT_DEBTOR_IBAN=
COBALT_DEBTOR_BIC=

# ── LLM gateway (LiteLLM) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Point at your LiteLLM instance. LLM_DEFAULT_MODEL defaults to iguana/gemma4-31b
# if unset. Set LLM_ESCALATION_MODEL to show an escalation toggle in the chat UI.

LLM_BASE_URL=https://llm-api.d-ma.be
DMABE_LLMAPI_KEY=
LLM_DEFAULT_MODEL=iguana/gemma4-31b
LLM_ESCALATION_MODEL=
